<!-- Voxel geometry drawn by its baked vertex-color light and blended by its
     texture's alpha: water, and anything else a game gave a real alpha to.
     The same shader as VoxelUnlit, with the cutoff replaced by a blend.

     It writes no depth, so it cannot sort against itself within one drawable;
     that is why the mesher puts these faces on a child node of their own,
     which Urho3D then sorts back to front against the other chunks'. There
     is no light pass: an additive pass over a blended one would double the
     translucent surface's contribution.

     Culling is off, which is what makes a water surface be there when the
     camera is under it. It is also why a translucent shape must not be built
     from doubled quads: a blended quad drawn twice blends twice and reads as
     opaque.
-->
<technique vs="luanti_client/res/VoxelUnlit" ps="luanti_client/res/VoxelUnlit"
        psdefines="TRANSLUCENT">
    <pass name="alpha" blend="alpha" depthwrite="false" cull="none" />
</technique>
